From: MacGameVideoFix <https://github.com/MathiasKowoll/MacGameVideoFix>
Subject: [PATCH] winebus.sys: present a DualSense on Bluetooth as if it were on USB, on request

WHERE THIS CAME FROM

MacGameVideoFix's own series, mgvf-NNNN, written on 2026-09-08 after
mgvf-0002, 0003 and 0004 had made the truth about the bus reachable -- and it
turned out that two consumers do not want the truth. Nothing in it is
specific to this project; it is offered to anyone carrying a patched winebus.

THE FAULT

Sony's libScePad (1.0.4.1, the copy two current titles ship) decides USB
against Bluetooth from the HidP capabilities alone: feature value caps on
usage page 0xFF00 mean the USB descriptor, 0xFF80 the Bluetooth one, and it
wants InputReportByteLength and FeatureReportByteLength of at least 64 with
OutputReportByteLength no more than 48 for a 0x0ce6 pad or 64 for a 0x0df2.
Having found the Bluetooth descriptor it still writes output report 0x02
only, the USB format; the Bluetooth descriptor has no 0x02, hidclass refuses
the write before winebus sees it, and the library drops the pad. Measured on
2026-09-08 with a +hid trace under CrossOver 26.3. Steam, for its part,
decides from hidapi's bus flag -- correct now, thanks to the three patches
before this one -- and from the title's store categories: 57, "PS5
controller support", without 58, "PS5 support over Bluetooth", puts up the
"plug in your controller" dialog whenever the flag says Bluetooth. Both are
content with a pad that looks wired.

THE CHANGE

An opt-in, per device, off by default. With "UsbEmulation" set under
HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\winebus\Devices\<vid>/<pid>, a
DualSense (054c:0ce6 or 054c:0df2) arriving over Bluetooth is created with
bus type USB -- so get_device_id, mgvf-0002's compatible ids and hidapi's
flag all say USB -- and at start its report descriptor is replaced by the USB
one of the pad, with HidP_GetCollectionDescription re-run on it. Both pads'
USB descriptors are built in, the plain one's 289 bytes and the Edge's 405,
each read from that pad on a cable; nothing is derived from the other's. From
then on every report is translated at the boundary, so the pad still gets what
it expects:

  input    0x31/78 -> 0x01/64  the USB report is bytes 2..64 of the Bluetooth
                               one; the nine reserved bytes and the CRC are
                               dropped.
           0x01/10 -> 0x01/64  the short report a paired pad sends until a
                               feature report has been read from it, spread
                               into the USB layout: sticks at 1..4, triggers
                               at 5..6, buttons at 8..10, the rest zero -- the
                               inverse of HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_BT's shuffle.
                               Feature report 0x05 is read once at start so
                               the pad leaves that mode, as SDL and the Linux
                               driver do.
           anything else longer than the descriptor declares for its id is
                               dropped: deliver_next_report copies it into
                               hidclass's read buffer without a bound, and
                               that buffer is now 64 bytes.
  output   0x02/N  -> 0x31/78  byte 1 the sequence number << 4, byte 2 0x10,
                               the N-1 data bytes from 3, zeros, and a CRC-32
                               over the seed byte 0xA2 then bytes 0..73,
                               little-endian at 74..77 -- through a packet of
                               our own, with IoStatus.Information set back to
                               the client's length. The counter lives in the
                               device extension. N is whatever the presented
                               descriptor declares -- 48 on a plain pad, 64 on
                               an Edge -- and the difference is only how many
                               zeros follow: the 0x31 is 78 bytes either way
                               and the effects block sits at the same offsets,
                               which is why one translation serves both.
  feature GET                  forwarded; the last four bytes of the report --
                               its declared length, not the client's buffer --
                               are zeroed, where the pad puts its CRC and a
                               wired pad returns zeros -- except in the
                               calibration report 0x05, which a wired pad
                               returns with the CRC in place, so that one goes
                               through untouched.
  feature SET                  forwarded from a copy with a CRC-32, seed byte
                               0x53, in its last four bytes. libScePad's own
                               Bluetooth path writes feature 0x08 that way,
                               and a failed write is fatal to its reader
                               thread.

A new flag, HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB, carries the decision from
bus_create_hid_device onwards; it excludes HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_BT, and
hidraw_disable_report_fixups leaves it alone, because a client of the
emulation never writes a Bluetooth report -- its first write is the reason
the emulation exists, not a reason to stop. The byte layouts and the
descriptor table are in a new file, dualsense_usb.c, with no kernel or wine
includes so that it compiles on the host: the CRC comes in as a function
pointer, RtlComputeCrc32 from the driver and zlib's crc32 from the test.

HOW THE LAYOUTS WERE MEASURED

From +hid traces of the same DualSense Edge, wired and paired. Its USB
descriptor, 405 bytes, is the one hidparse dumped from the wired pad, twice
and byte-identical. The plain DualSense's, 289 bytes, was read on 2026-09-08
from that pad on a cable through IOKit's kIOHIDReportDescriptorKey -- the
property winebus itself reads -- and differs from the Edge's in three items
and no others: output 0x02 of 47 data bytes rather than 63, feature 0xf2 of 16
bytes rather than 53, and no profile reports 0x60..0x7b. The input report is
the same, which is why the input translations were not re-measured for it. The 0x31 packing was checked byte for byte, CRC included,
against two output reports Steam itself wrote to the paired pad (CRCs
231501b5 and 808dbf02) and against a packing sent through IOKit with no wine
involved, which rumbled the pad. The feature reports that exist in both
descriptors -- 0x05/41, 0x08/48, 0x09/20, 0x20/64 among them -- have the
same length in both, which is what lets the CRC tail be addressed by the
declared length. The tail rule itself comes from the same pad's reads of 0x09
and 0x20, byte-identical wired and paired except for those four bytes -- a
seed-0xA3 CRC paired, zeros wired -- and from its 0x05, which came back
identical on both transports, CRC included. The host test, usb_emu_test.c,
lives beside the descriptors in the build directory and asserts all of the
above against zlib, the trace bytes included.

THE OPTION

  HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\winebus\Devices\<vid>/<pid>
    UsbEmulation  REG_DWORD  0 (default): as before. Non-zero: present the
                             pad as if on USB.
    ProductId     REG_DWORD  0 (default): keep the pad's own. Otherwise the
                             product id to present, e.g. 0x0ce6 for an Edge,
                             for a client that knows only the plain pad.

Both are read when the pad arrives, on the bus thread, from the key named
after the pad's real ids. A ProductId whose USB descriptor is not in the
table is ignored with a warning, and so is UsbEmulation itself for a pad
whose own descriptor is not: nothing is ever presented with a descriptor
derived from another pad's.

NOT COVERED

A DualShock 4, and any other pad: deliberately. Its report descriptor, its
input reports and its output reports all differ from a DualSense's, so it
would be a second translation layer rather than another row in the table, and
nothing measured asks for one. Both DualSense ids are served.

The driver half has not run against a live pad. Everything above about the
byte layouts is proved against captured bytes by the host test, and the
0x31 packing rumbled the pad through IOKit with no wine involved; but the
option has not yet been turned on under a running title, so the first use
belongs in a +hid trace.

The pad's own CRC on its 0x31 input (seed 0xA1) is not
verified; SDL and the Linux driver drop a report that fails it. Feature ids
that exist only in the USB descriptor (0x0a, 0x0c, 0x21, 0x84, 0x85, 0xa0,
0xe0) fail at the pad, as they would on a wired pad without them. Whether a
wired pad zeros the tail of any feature report other than 0x09 and 0x20 has
not been measured; the rule is applied to every read but 0x05.

RISKS

A client that does know the pad is on Bluetooth -- Steam for a title with
category 58, SDL -- now sees a wired pad and writes 0x02 instead of 0x31; the
translation carries the same effects block, so nothing is lost, and the
option is per device and off by default. The descriptor swap happens under
the device's critical section at start, before the last-report buffers are
sized from it, and only if the new descriptor parses; otherwise the start
fails with the parse status rather than running with a descriptor the
translations do not match. The one-shot feature read at start is synchronous
on the PnP thread, like any feature read a client makes. Feature 0xf5 is 4
bytes in the USB descriptor and 8 in the Bluetooth one, the one size that
differs among the shared ids: a client writing it gets a CRC in the USB
tail, which the pad will refuse. RtlComputeCrc32 is one more import from
ntoskrnl.exe.

